 Many difficult problems faced in robotics and AI involve large multi-agent systems, such as networks of autonomous vehicles, drone swarms, factory robot teams, or network routing. Recently, multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) has shown promise on many of these problems, by enabling highly complex and cooperative strategies to be automatically learned, as opposed to manually programmed. For example, MARL algorithms have achieved super-human performance on complex multi-agent strategy games such as  Starcraft II \cite{arxivID-2012.13169-0} and Dota 2 \cite{arxivID-1912.06680-1}. However, despite this progress, MARL algorithms still struggle with many relatively simple tasks, such as cooperative navigation \cite{arxivID-2006.07869-2}, and have not yet been scaled to problems with more than a few dozen agents \cite{arxivID-2209.03859-3}.

One of the key obstacles to scaling MARL to large numbers of agents is the credit assignment problem \cite{arxivID-2307.08171-4}. In MARL, the reward received by each agent is often a function of the actions taken by all agents, and the credit assignment problem is to determine the degree to which each agent is responsible for the reward. This is difficult in large-scale multi-agent systems, because the credit assignment problem is compounded by the curse of dimensionality, and because agents actions may be highly correlated.

In this paper, we address the credit assignment problem with an approach that we call partial reward decoupling (PRD). In PRD, we assume that a subset of agents in a multi-agent system can be identified, such that the reward received by each agent in this subset is a function of the actions taken by only this subset of agents. By assuming that agents are decoupled in this way, we can treat the reward received by each agent as if it were the only agent in the system, thereby simplifying the credit assignment problem. We show that by using PRD, we can decompose a large cooperative MARL problem into a set of smaller decoupled subproblems, each of which can be solved independently. We then show that this approach can be used in an actor-critic algorithm, and that the resulting algorithm outperforms various other actor-critic approaches on a wide array of cooperative multi-agent RL tasks. We also relate our approach to counterfactual multi-agent policy gradient (COMA), a state-of-the-art MARL algorithm, and empirically show that our approach outperforms COMA by making better use of information in agents reward streams, and by enabling recent advances in advantage estimation to be used.

In summary, the contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We propose a novel approach to decomposing large cooperative multi-agent RL problems into smaller decoupled subproblems, using an assumption that some subset of agents can be identified such that the reward received by each agent in this subset is a function of the actions taken by only this subset of agents.
\item We show that this approach can be used in an actor-critic algorithm, and that the resulting algorithm outperforms various other actor-critic approaches on a wide array of cooperative multi-agent RL tasks.
\item We relate our approach to counterfactual multi-agent policy gradient (COMA), a state-of-the-art MARL algorithm, and empirically show that our approach outperforms COMA by making better use of information in agents reward streams, and by enabling recent advances in advantage estimation to be used.
\end{enumerate}

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related_work} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:partial_reward_decoupling} introduces partial reward decoupling, and Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} describes our algorithm. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} presents experimental results, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
Related Work
\label{sec:related_work}
Many algorithms have been proposed for solving multi-agent RL problems, including value-based methods \cite{arxivID-2406.07848-5, arxivID-2104.09343-6, arxivID-2302.01188-7}, policy-based methods \cite{arxivID-1408.1484-8, arxivID-2004.08883-9, arxivID-2011.00382-10}, and actor-critic methods \cite{arxivID-1706.02275-11, arxivID-1810.02912-12, arxivID-2004.11145-13}. However, these algorithms often struggle with the credit assignment problem, which is a key obstacle to scaling MARL to large numbers of agents \cite{arxivID-2302.05007-14}. To address this problem, many algorithms have been proposed that use some form of decoupling, including independent Q-learning \cite{arxivID-2202.02349-15}, independent learning \cite{arxivID-2111.01100-16}, and independent policy gradient \cite{arxivID-2405.06161-17}. However, these algorithms often fail to learn cooperative behavior, as they do not take into account the actions of other agents when assigning credit \cite{arxivID-0904.2320-18}. To address this issue, many algorithms have been proposed that use some form of centralized training, including value decomposition methods \cite{arxivID-1706.05296-19, arxivID-2408.15381-20, arxivID-2109.10632-21}, and policy gradient methods \cite{arxivID-1705.08926-22}

