 With the development of mobile devices, huge quantities and diverse types of data have been generated, which promote the utilization of machine learning technologies. However, when aggregated to central model training, data with sensitive privacy can lead to serious privacy leakage. To address the privacy challenges, federated learning (FL) has been proposed to aggregate local model parameters,  which avoids direct data transmission \cite{arxivID-1902.04885-0}. The local datasets in FL are kept on mobile devices and only model parameters are transmitted to the server. Then, the server aggregates the local models and sends the global model back to the local devices. The FL training process can be repeated until the global model converges. However, FL faces challenges in both data and model reliability, which can cause performance degradation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figure/illustration.pdf}
\caption{A defective scenario in FL.}
\label{illustration}
\end{figure}

The data reliability in FL can be affected by various defects, including data poisoning \cite{arxivID-2007.08432-1}, label noise \cite{arxivID-2012.01700-2}, and data heterogeneity \cite{arxivID-2011.06393-3}. As shown in Figure~\ref{illustration}, data poisoning can be caused by malicious local clients, which is likely to cause a global model that is significantly worse than the local models. Label noise may be caused by automatic image recognition or incorrect labeling by users. The local datasets with label noise can lead to a global model that is inferior to the local models. In addition, the data heterogeneity can also cause performance degradation in the global model.

To address the problems of data reliability in FL, many methods have been proposed. For example, data poisoning can be detected by a classifier \cite{arxivID-2201.13086-4}, and a robust aggregation scheme can be used to alleviate the impact of data poisoning \cite{arxivID-2102.05257-5}. However, these methods cannot be applied to label noise and data heterogeneity. To address label noise, a global-guided pseudo-labeling method can be used to update the labels of unconfident samples \cite{arxivID-2012.01700-6}. To address data heterogeneity, some studies have explored the use of domain adaptation methods to align local models \cite{arxivID-1911.02054-7}. However, these methods cannot be applied to other defects. In addition, the existing methods do not consider the effects of defects on the model quality and only focus on the impact of defects on the data.

For the model reliability in FL, model aggregation is widely used to improve the performance of the global model. However, the models with different quality should be assigned different weights to improve the performance of the global model. The weight of each local model can be determined by the model quality. For example, a method of assigning different weights to local models based on the gradient similarity is proposed \cite{arxivID-2003.02089-8}. However, this method does not consider the impact of defects. In addition, the weight of each local model can be dynamically assigned by the server to improve the performance of the global model. For example, a method based on the deep deterministic policy gradient algorithm is proposed to dynamically assign weights to local models \cite{arxivID-2211.02296-9}. However, this method is not suitable for the FL environment, as it cannot be trained in a decentralized manner. In addition, a method based on the soft actor-critic algorithm is proposed to dynamically assign weights to local models \cite{arxivID-2201.12701-10}. However, this method does not consider the impact of defects on the model quality, which may lead to poor performance.

In this paper, we propose the DEfect-AwaRe federated soft actor-critic (DearFSAC) to dynamically assign weights to local models to improve the robustness of FL. The deep reinforcement learning algorithm soft actor-critic is adopted for near-optimal performance and stable convergence. Besides, an auto-encoder is trained to output low-dimensional embedding vectors that are further utilized to evaluate model quality. In the experiments, DearFSAC outperforms three existing approaches on four datasets for both independent and identically distributed (IID) and non-IID settings under defective scenarios.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose the DEfect-AwaRe federated soft actor-critic (DearFSAC) to dynamically assign weights to local models to improve the robustness of FL.
\item We adopt the soft actor-critic algorithm for near-optimal performance and stable convergence.
\item We train an auto-encoder to output low-dimensional embedding vectors that are further utilized to evaluate model quality.
\item We evaluate DearFSAC on four datasets for both IID and non-IID settings under defective scenarios.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\subsection{Federated Learning}

Federated learning (FL) is a distributed machine learning framework that allows multiple parties to collaboratively train a model without sharing their private data \cite{arxivID-1511.03575-11}. The FL framework can be divided into three parts: the server, the local clients, and the communication channel. The server is responsible for coordinating the training process and aggregating the model updates from the local clients. The local clients are responsible for training the model on their local data and sending the model updates to the server. The communication channel is responsible for transmitting the model updates between the server and the local clients. The FL framework has several advantages over traditional machine learning frameworks, including improved privacy, improved scalability, and improved robustness.

\subsection{Model Aggregation}

Model aggregation is a technique used in FL to combine the local models trained on different devices into a single global model. The most common model aggregation method is weighted averaging, where the weights are determined by the amount of data used to train each local model. Other methods include geometric median, Krum, and Trimmed Mean \cite{arxivID-1912.13445-12}

