\subsection{Problem statement}

Consider the following discrete-time linear time-invariant system:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:1}
\begin{cases}
\mathbf{x}(k+1)=A\mathbf{x}(k)+B\mathbf{u}(k),\ k=0,1,\ldots,\\
\mathbf{x}(0)=\mathbf{x}_{0},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, $B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$ and $\mathbf{x}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are known, $\mathbf{x}(k)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\mathbf{u}(k)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$ are the state and control input at time $k$, respectively. We assume that the system is asymptotically stable, i.e. there exist $\rho>0$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$ such that $\|A^k\|\leq\rho\alpha^k$ for all $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$, where $\|\cdot\|$ denotes the Euclidean norm. We consider the following sampled-data system with time delays:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2}
\begin{cases}
\mathbf{x}(k+1)=A\mathbf{x}(k)+B\mathbf{u}(k),\ k=0,1,\ldots,\\
\mathbf{x}(0)=\mathbf{x}_{0},\\
\mathbf{u}(k)=\mathbf{u}_{k},\ k=0,1,\ldots,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{u}_{k}=\mathbf{u}(k_{k})$ and $k_{k}$ is the last sampling time prior to $k$, i.e., $k_{k}=k_{0}+k_{1}+\ldots+k_{n_{k}}$ with $k_{0}=0$, $k_{n_{k}}\leq k$ and $k_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$ for all $i=0,1,\ldots,n_{k}$. The objective of this paper is to design a controller for the system (\ref{eq:2}) such that the closed-loop system is asymptotically stable. The design of such a controller requires the knowledge of the system matrices $A$ and $B$. However, the matrices $A$ and $B$ are not always available in practice. This motivates us to design the controller based on data collected from the system (\ref{eq:2}). More specifically, we assume that the following data is available:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:3}
\begin{cases}
\mathbf{x}(k_{0})=\mathbf{x}_{0},\\
\mathbf{x}(k_{1}),\mathbf{u}(k_{1}),\\
\mathbf{x}(k_{2}),\mathbf{u}(k_{2}),\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{x}(k_{n_{k}}),\mathbf{u}(k_{n_{k}}),\\
\vdots
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $k_{0}=0$ and $k_{n_{k}}\leq k$ for all $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$. We aim to design a controller for the system (\ref{eq:2}) based on the data (\ref{eq:3}). The design of such a controller is challenging since the data (\ref{eq:3}) is collected from the system (\ref{eq:2}) with time delays and the sampling times $k_{0},k_{1},\ldots,k_{n_{k}},\ldots$ are unknown. In this paper, we will address this problem using the looped-functional approach.

