 The blow-up phenomenon in nonlinear evolution equations has been studied extensively in the literature. Some studies focus on physical applications, such as singularity formation in fluids #ref2# #ref3# #ref10# #ref14#, runaway in thermal processes #ref6# #ref9# #ref13#, and biological applications #ref11#. Others deal with numerical aspects such as the computation of blow-up profiles,  the computation of the blow-up time, and the continuation of the solution beyond the singularity #ref1# #ref4# #ref5# #ref7# #ref8# #ref12#.

The goal of this work is to provide a better understanding of the blow-up phenomenon in the one-dimensional semilinear heat equation
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:heat}
u_t = u_{xx} + f(u),
\end{equation}
with $f(u) = u^2/2$. The equation is supplemented with periodic boundary conditions and a positive, nearly flat initial condition. The blow-up phenomenon manifests itself in the fact that the solution becomes unbounded in finite time. The time of blow-up is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:blowup}
T = \frac{1}{\lambda}\left(1 - \exp\left(-\lambda\int_0^L u_0(x) dx\right)\right),
\end{equation}
where $\lambda = \max f'(u)$. The blow-up time is finite for all positive initial conditions, but the blow-up rate is slow for initial conditions that are close to zero.

The work is based on a combination of numerical and analytical tools. The numerical method is a spectral method with a time-stepping scheme based on a second-order Runge-Kutta scheme. The numerical results are used to obtain asymptotic approximations for the solution and the blow-up profile that are valid in the entire space and time interval. The approximations are in good agreement with the numerical results and provide insight into the blow-up phenomenon.

The paper is organized as follows. The numerical method is presented in Section~\ref{sec:num}. The blow-up profile is studied in Section~\ref{sec:profile}. The asymptotic approximations for the solution and the blow-up profile are presented in Section~\ref{sec:asymp}. The results are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:disc}. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:cont} presents preliminary results on continuing the numerical solution beyond the singularity. <|section_end|>

