 Although neural networks provide state of the art performance for image classification problems, including those in medical imaging  #ref6# #ref14# #ref15#, their success is mostly limited to problems with predefined classes that are supported by a large volume of labelled training data. However, in many settings, there are a significant number of diagnostic classes that occur infrequently, making it difficult to assemble fully labeled training data sets. Instead the data from these rare classes is  often available in the form of unlabelled data, e.g., because the patients with these conditions are hard to find. Moreover, there are many more conditions than are currently diagnosed. Thus, it is important to develop methods that can detect the presence of unseen classes in the data. We call such a problem Out-of-Distribution (OOD) detection.

OOD detection is an active area of research in computer vision \cite{arxivID-2110.11334-0}. The recent survey by \cite{arxivID-2409.11884-1}, gives an overview of the approaches that can be broadly categorized into training-driven and training-agnostic. In training-driven approaches, the model learns to classify in-distribution (ID) data while learning to detect OOD samples by imposing an additional constraint \cite{arxivID-1711.09325-2, arxivID-2007.06096-3}. In contrast, training-agnostic methods use a trained model to detect OOD samples. Training-agnostic methods can be further categorized as density estimation based \cite{arxivID-2006.09273-4}, distance based \cite{arxivID-2204.06507-5}, and classification based \cite{arxivID-2310.18104-6}. Although OOD detection has been well studied in computer vision, there are limited works showing the efficacy of unsupervised OOD methods on complex medical data. In this work, we present preliminary findings of our unsupervised OOD detection algorithm, SimCLR-LOF, as well as a recent state of the art approach (SSD), applied on medical images. SimCLR-LOF learns semantically meaningful features using SimCLR \cite{arxivID-2002.05709-7} and uses LOF \cite{arxivID-0903.3257-8} for scoring if a test sample is OOD. We evaluated on the multi-source International Skin Imaging Collaboration (ISIC) 2019 dataset \cite{arxivID-1902.03368-9}, and show results that are competitive with SSD as well as with recent supervised approaches applied on the same data.

