In this paper, we consider a new MP-MAB problem with shareable arms, in which several plays can share the same arm. For instance, in a 5G network, a base station can serve multiple users, and each user can be considered as a play \cite{arxivID-1509.00338-8}. This problem can be formulated as a MP-MAB problem with shareable arms, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:MP-MAB}, where each play is a user and each arm is a base station. In this problem, each play can only pull one arm per time slot, and the learner can choose several plays to share the same arm. When the number of plays sharing an arm is less than or equal to the arm's capacity, each play can receive the same reward as in the canonical MP-MAB problem. Otherwise, each play receives the reward from a load-dependent distribution, which is the per-load reward multiplying the number of plays. We model the per-load reward distribution as a Gaussian distribution with unknown mean and variance. This problem is different from the MP-MAB problem with shareable arms \cite{arxivID-2206.08776-9}, in which the learner knows the per-load reward distribution.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows: (1) We formulate the MP-MAB problem with shareable arms, in which each play can pull one arm and the learner can choose several plays to share the same arm. (2) We prove a sample complexity lower bound of learning the capacity from load-dependent rewards and also a regret lower bound of this new MP-MAB problem. (3) We devise a capacity estimator whose sample complexity upper bound matches the lower bound in terms of reward means and capacities. (4) We propose an online learning algorithm to address the problem and prove its regret upper bound. This regret upper bounds first term is the same as regret lower bounds, and its second and third terms also evidently correspond to lower bounds. (5) We conduct extensive experiments to validate our algorithms' performance and also its gain in 5G \& 4G base station selection.

