\subsection{Lifelong Reinforcement Learning}
LRL is an emerging area of research at the intersection of deep RL, meta-learning, and continual learning. LRL is a generalization of the classical RL problem in which the agent is trained on a stream of tasks, with no prior knowledge of the task sequence. The agent must learn to perform well on all tasks, while retaining the ability to adapt to new tasks. This is in contrast to the classical RL setting, in which the agent is trained on a single task. LRL is a challenging problem, as the agent must be able to learn a wide range of tasks, while retaining the ability to adapt to new tasks. There are a number of approaches to LRL, including meta-learning, transfer learning, and continual learning. Meta-learning approaches aim to learn a general-purpose learner that can quickly adapt to new tasks. Transfer learning approaches aim to transfer knowledge from previous tasks to new tasks. Continual learning approaches aim to learn a sequence of tasks, while retaining the ability to adapt to new tasks. In this work, we focus on the meta-learning approach to LRL, as it is the most general and flexible. 

