 Advances in computer vision technologies have enabled numerous healthcare-related applications covering from automated disease diagnosis, ambient health monitoring to assistive/therapeutic rehabilitation  #ref1#. Especially, vision-based markerless human motion capture algorithms play an important role in biomechanical analysis and neurological disease diagnosis. The body skeletons derived from these algorithms can provide macro-level motion trajectories characterizing activities of daily living  (ADL), such as walking, running, jumping, etc. However, the existing methods are mainly developed based on RGB images, which are vulnerable to the illumination changes and occlusions, and they are sensitive to the viewpoint changes \cite{arxivID-1709.05087-0}. In contrast, depth images provide robust 3D geometric information with less sensitivity to illumination changes and occlusions, and they are less affected by the viewpoint changes. The depth images can be easily captured by depth cameras, such as the Microsoft Kinect. However, the existing pose estimation models for depth images are still limited by the inherent problems of the depth images, i.e. occlusions and viewpoint changes. Occlusions occur when the depth camera fails to capture the complete body surface. The occluded regions will not be represented in the depth images. Furthermore, the depth images exhibit heterogeneous characteristics under different viewpoints. It is therefore important to develop pose estimation algorithms for depth images that can generalize well across different viewpoints and are robust to occlusions.

