\caption{The proposed framework for cross-view generalization. The backbone network is rotation-equivariant and occlusion-invariant. The rotation-equivariance is achieved by the rotation-equivariant convolution layer and the occlusion-invariance is achieved by the occlusion-invariant loss. The model is trained with real-world data from a single view and unlabelled synthetic data from multiple views. It can generalize well on the real-world data from all the other unseen views.}
\label{figure2}
\end{figure*}

In this paper, we propose a novel approach for cross-view generalization with an occlusion-invariant semi-supervised learning framework built upon a novel rotation-equivariant backbone. Our approach is trained with real-world data from a single view and unlabelled synthetic data from multiple views. It can generalize well on the real-world data from all the other unseen views. The proposed framework consists of three main components: (1) an occlusion-invariant semi-supervised learning framework, (2) a rotation-equivariant backbone, and (3) an occlusion-invariant loss. The occlusion-invariant semi-supervised learning framework consists of a teacher network and a student network. The teacher network is trained with real-world data from a single view and unlabelled synthetic data from multiple views. The student network is trained with the real-world data from the single view. The student network is encouraged to learn from the teacher network by minimizing the discrepancy between their outputs. The rotation-equivariant backbone is built upon the rotation-equivariant convolution layer. The rotation-equivariance is achieved by the rotation-equivariant convolution layer and the occlusion-invariance is achieved by the occlusion-invariant loss. The occlusion-invariant loss is defined as the difference between the output of the model and the output of the model with the occluded regions. The occlusion-invariant loss is used to regularize the model to be robust to occlusions. The occlusion-invariant semi-supervised learning framework is used to train the rotation-equivariant backbone. The proposed framework is evaluated on our ICL-Gait dataset and ITOP dataset. The results show that our approach outperforms the state-of-the-arts on gait analysis and can produce more convincing keypoints on ITOP dataset, than its provided ground truth.

