 Object detection is a fundamental task in the field of computer vision which predicts the objects with locations and pre-defined categories in a given image. It has far reaching impact on wide practical applications include intelligent surveillance, autonomous driving, etc. With the development of deep neural networks, the performance of object detection has been improved by different  approaches, e.g., anchor-based \cite{arxivID-1506.01497-0, arxivID-1506.01497-1}, anchor-free \cite{arxivID-2104.04899-2} and transformer-based \cite{arxivID-2110.03921-3, arxivID-2203.16527-4} methods, etc. However, the performance of these methods is largely dependent on the sample assignment strategy which determines the positive and negative samples for each anchor or pixel. Therefore, a well-designed sample assignment scheme is crucial for the training of object detection models.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{./figs/fig1.pdf}
\caption{The framework of the proposed HPS-Det. It first defines the hyper-parameters $m$ as the number of positive samples assigned to each ground truth. Then, a surrogate optimization algorithm is used to derive the optimal choices of hyper-parameters $m^*$. Finally, a dynamic sample assignment procedure is designed to dynamically select the optimal number of positives at each training iteration.}
\label{fig1}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{./figs/fig2.pdf}
\caption{The comparison between the existing sample assignment schemes and our proposed dynamic sample assignment scheme. (a) Existing sample assignment schemes: (a1) all positive samples are assigned to each ground truth; (a2) only one positive sample is assigned to each ground truth; (a3) the number of positive samples is fixed for each ground truth. (b) Our dynamic sample assignment scheme.}
\label{fig2}
\end{figure*}

In the existing literature, the sample assignment schemes can be roughly divided into two categories, i.e., one-to-one assignment and one-to-many assignment. In the one-to-one assignment, each ground truth is assigned with only one positive sample. For example, in Faster R-CNN \cite{arxivID-1706.01061-5}, each anchor is assigned with a confidence score, and only the anchor with the highest confidence score is assigned as the positive sample for each ground truth. In the one-to-many assignment, each ground truth is assigned with multiple positive samples. For example, in YOLO \cite{arxivID-2001.00361-6}, each grid cell is assigned with multiple positive samples and each positive sample is assigned with multiple anchors. These methods assign the positive samples by a fixed strategy, e.g., the anchor with the highest confidence score or the anchor with the highest IoU with the ground truth. However, the sample assignment scheme is manually designed and cannot adapt to the changes of training status, which may lead to sub-optimal performance.

To address this issue, we propose a novel dynamic sample assignment scheme based on hyper-parameter search. We first define the number of positive samples assigned to each ground truth as the hyper-parameters and employ a surrogate optimization algorithm to derive the optimal choices. Then, we design a dynamic sample assignment procedure to dynamically select the optimal number of positives at each training iteration. Our proposed method is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig1}. Specifically, we first define the number of positive samples assigned to each ground truth as the hyper-parameters, and then employ a surrogate optimization algorithm to derive the optimal choices of hyper-parameters. Finally, we design a dynamic sample assignment procedure to dynamically select the optimal number of positives at each training iteration. We compare our proposed method with the existing sample assignment schemes in Figure \ref{fig2}. Our method can dynamically adjust the number of positive samples assigned to each ground truth according to the training status, which can adapt to the changes of training status and improve the performance of object detection.

The contributions of this work are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a novel dynamic sample assignment scheme based on hyper-parameter search, which can dynamically adjust the number of positive samples assigned to each ground truth according to the training status, and improve the performance of object detection.
\item We design a dynamic sample assignment procedure to dynamically select the optimal number of positives at each training iteration, which can adapt to the changes of training status and improve the performance of object detection.
\item We analyze the hyper-parameter reusability when transferring between different datasets and between different backbones for object detection, which exhibits the superiority and versatility of our method.
\end{itemize}
