 Automatic summarization frameworks condense an input document into shorter text consisting of the main points in that document. Neural networks have achieved state of the art results for both paradigms of abstractive summarization #ref19# #ref4# and extractive summarization  #ref15# #ref18#. While extractive models are factually more consistent with the content in  the input document, abstractive models have the advantage of being able to generate new words that are not present in the input document.  However, in order to generate new words, abstractive models need to have an external knowledge source like Wikipedia \cite{arxivID-2006.15435-0} or BERT \cite{arxivID-1808.07913-1}.

The summarization literature focuses on the summarization of news articles. The news articles in the CNN-DailyMail are relatively short documents with about 30 sentences per document on average. We introduce SciBERTSUM, our summarization framework designed for the summarization of long documents like scientific papers with more than 500 sentences. SciBERTSUM extends BERTSUM to long documents by 1) adding a section embedding layer to include section information in the sentence vector and 2) applying a sparse attention mechanism where each sentences will attend locally to nearby sentences and only a small number of sentences attend globally to all other sentences. We used slides generated by the authors of scientific papers as reference summaries since they contain the technical details from the paper. The results show the superiority of our model in terms of ROUGE scores.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/scibertsum.pdf}
\caption{The architecture of SciBERTSUM.}
\label{fig:scibertsum}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figures/scibertsum2.pdf}
\caption{The architecture of SciBERTSUM with sparse attention.}
\label{fig:scibertsum2}
\end{figure}
Related Work
\label{sec:related}

The most common summarization framework is based on the encoder-decoder architecture. The encoder processes the input document and produces a sequence of hidden states. The decoder takes the output of the encoder and generates the summary. There are two paradigms for summarization based on the type of decoder used. In abstractive summarization, the decoder is a neural language model, while in extractive summarization, the decoder is a sequence classifier. 

In the abstractive setting, the decoder is trained to generate a sequence of words that is similar to the ground truth summary. There are two main approaches to abstractive summarization. The first approach is based on an auto-regressive sequence model where the decoder generates one word at a time \cite{arxivID-1509.00685-2}. The second approach is based on a seq2seq model with an attention mechanism \cite{arxivID-2404.08685-3}. 

In the extractive setting, the decoder is a sequence classifier that predicts a binary label for each input sentence. The model outputs the sentences with the highest probability of being in the summary. The main advantage of the extractive approach is that it is factually more consistent with the input document. There are two main approaches to extractive summarization. The first approach is based on a supervised learning framework where the model is trained to predict whether a sentence should be included in the summary \cite{arxivID-1611.04244-4}. The second approach is based on a sequence-to-sequence model with an attention mechanism \cite{arxivID-1907.03491-5}. <|section_end|>

 Modern deep learning methods can provide high accuracy in settings where the model is evaluated on data from the same distribution as the training set, but accuracy often degrades severely when there is a mismatch between the training and test distributions  #ref18# #ref47#. In safety-critical settings, effectively deploying machine learning models requires not only high accuracy, but also requires the model to reliably quantify uncertainty in its predictions in order to assess risk and potentially abstain from making dangerous, unreliable predictions. Reliably estimating uncertainty is especially important in settings with distribution shift where inaccurate predictions are more prevalent, but the  model may be less confident in them.  In this work, we address the problem of how to adapt a deep neural network to a new distribution shift at test time.  We propose a Bayesian model that provides a well-defined relationship between unlabeled test inputs and model parameters, and show how approximate inference in this model can be instantiated with a simple regularized entropy minimization procedure at test-time. We evaluate our method on a variety of distribution shifts for image classification, including image corruptions, natural distribution shifts, and domain adaptation settings, and show that our method improves both accuracy and uncertainty estimation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/teaser.pdf}
\caption{Illustration of the proposed approach for adapting a model to a new test distribution. During training, the model is trained on a labeled dataset from a source distribution. At test time, we observe unlabeled inputs from a new target distribution, and use them to adapt the model to the target distribution.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]{figures/model.pdf}
\caption{Bayesian model for test-time adaptation. We consider a setting where we have a trained model $f_\theta$, and would like to adapt it to a new test distribution. At test time, we observe unlabeled inputs from the test distribution, and use them to infer the model parameters $\theta^*$ that would have been obtained if we had trained the model on labeled data from the test distribution. We use the posterior $p(\theta^* \mid x)$ to adapt the model to the test distribution.}
\label{fig:model}
\end{figure*}
Related Work
\textbf{Adapting to distribution shift at test time.}  One approach to adapting to distribution shift at test-time is to use methods that modify the model weights after training. \cite{arxivID-1802.10542-0} proposed a method that stores a memory of training examples and then uses a context-based lookup to directly modify the weights of a neural network. \cite{arxivID-1707.01461-1} proposed a method that organizes the memory with classes as the primary key, and acts as a second boosted stage following a regular neural network. \cite{arxivID-1705.04228-2} proposed a method that constrains newly learned filters to be linear combinations of existing ones. \cite{arxivID-1905.10698-3} proposed a method that maximizes the entropy of the output estimates initially and stalling the first back-propagated error at the output of the last layer. \cite{arxivID-1811.01990-4} proposed a method that encourages structured sparsity in the set of offset tensors during learning via group lasso regularization. \cite{arxivID-1906.00408-5} proposed a method that adaptively ensembles multiple models trained on different domains. \cite{arxivID-2103.02205-6} proposed a method that gradually fine-tunes in a multi-stage process. \cite{arxivID-2112.02359-7} proposed a method that updates only the normalization parameters of the network with the unlabeled target data. \cite{arxivID-2010.01926-8} proposed a method that adapts to a specific target subject from the target domain. \cite{arxivID-2007.10233-9} proposed a method that adapts a source classifier to a target domain that varies from the source domain along natural axes, such as brightness and contrast. \cite{arxivID-2109.12746-10} proposed a method that adapts a model to unlabeled inputs from the test distribution. \cite{arxivID-2007.02931-11} proposed a method that optimizes models for effective adaptation to distribution shift by learning to adapt on the training domains. \cite{arxivID-2212.05987-12} proposed a method that minimizes the dropout variance, an approximation of Bayesian Predictive uncertainty. \cite{arxivID-2106.03762-13} proposed a method that exposes the original model to corrupted images, and performs simple statistical calibration on the image outputs. \cite{arxivID-2106.14999-14} proposed a method that adds a diversity regularizer based on batch-wise entropy maximization that prevents convergence to trivial collapsed solutions. \cite{arxivID-2207.11707-15} proposed a method that adjusts the model pre-trained on the source domain using only unlabeled online data from the target domain. \cite{arxivID-2310.04941-16} proposed a method that leverages the agreement-on-the-line phenomenon to make TTA methods more reliable. \cite{arxivID-2408.07511-17} proposed a method that detects distribution shifts in the classifier's entropy values obtained on a stream of unlabeled samples. \cite{arxivID-2407.12128-18}

