The flow-based generative models \cite{arxivID-1908.09257-0}, such as the RealNVP \cite{arxivID-1605.08803-1}, the Glow \cite{arxivID-1807.03039-2}, the Flow++ \cite{arxivID-1902.00275-3}, and the FFJORD \cite{arxivID-1810.01367-4}, have shown an excellent ability to learn the probability density function of data. Their main idea is to learn a sequence of invertible transformations to transform a simple distribution (e.g., the Gaussian distribution) to a complex distribution of data, such as images. The invertible transformations are implemented by neural networks, which can be trained by optimizing the log-likelihood objective. The learned transformations can be used for sampling and density estimation.

The success of the flow-based models lies in their invertibility. To enable the invertibility, they usually adopt a coupling-based strategy \cite{arxivID-1804.06203-5}, which splits the input data into two parts. One part is directly passed through the network, and the other part is transformed by the network. This strategy results in a tractable Jacobian determinant, which is the product of the Jacobian determinants of the two parts. The coupling-based strategy is implemented by a sequence of coupling layers, where each coupling layer contains a coupling unit. The coupling unit consists of a permutation of the input variables and an affine transformation of the permuted variables. The permutation is implemented by a permutation matrix, which is a binary matrix with exactly one 1 in each row and each column. The permutation matrix is obtained by applying a permutation function to the input variables. The affine transformation is implemented by a neural network, which transforms the permuted variables to new variables. The permutation function and the neural network are learned during training.

Although the flow-based models have shown promising results, they still face challenges in modeling the long-range data dependencies. The coupling-based strategy splits the input data into two parts, and the permutation matrix is a binary matrix. Therefore, the permutation function is not able to capture the long-range dependencies between the input variables. The neural network in the affine transformation is also not able to capture the long-range dependencies between the input variables, since it only transforms a small part of the input variables. To address this issue, we propose to introduce attention mechanisms into the flow-based models. Attention mechanisms have been widely used in various domains, such as natural language processing \cite{arxivID-2404.08685-6, arxivID-2408.04216-7}, computer vision \cite{arxivID-2106.07550-8, arxivID-2301.11022-9}, and speech recognition \cite{arxivID-2102.07259-10, arxivID-2205.14329-11}. They have shown their capability of capturing long-range dependencies between the input variables. However, attention mechanisms are not commonly used in flow-based models, due to the invertibility requirement.

