Related Work
\paragraph{Scaling Law}
The scaling law refers to the relationship between the amount of computation and the performance of a model. It has been observed that the performance of a model improves with the increase in the amount of computation, and this relationship can be described by a power-law function. This phenomenon has been observed in various domains, including language modeling \cite{arxivID-2001.08361-0}, vision recognition \cite{arxivID-2106.04560-1}, and multimodal learning \cite{arxivID-2409.06754-2}. In this paper, we investigate the scaling law in user representation learning and show that the training error scales as a power-law with the amount of computation.

\paragraph{User Representation Learning}
User representation learning aims to learn a low-dimensional representation of users from their historical interactions. This representation can be used to improve the performance of various downstream tasks, such as recommendation, content understanding, and user behavior modeling. There are two main approaches to user representation learning: collaborative filtering and content-based methods. Collaborative filtering methods learn user representations by modeling the user-item interaction matrix, while content-based methods learn user representations by modeling the user's historical interactions with items. Recently, some works have proposed to combine collaborative filtering and content-based methods to learn a better user representation \cite{arxivID-1905.13130-3, arxivID-2010.07050-4, arxivID-1804.06201-5, arxivID-2204.00849-6}. However, these methods are not scalable to large-scale user data due to the high computational cost of training and inference.

\paragraph{Contrastive Learning}
Contrastive learning is a self-supervised learning method that learns representations by maximizing the similarity between positive pairs and minimizing the similarity between negative pairs. It has been successfully applied to various domains, including computer vision \cite{arxivID-1911.05722-7, arxivID-2003.04297-8, arxivID-2009.00104-9}, natural language processing \cite{arxivID-2005.00669-10, arxivID-2109.05424-11, arxivID-2106.07345-12}, and multimodal learning \cite{arxivID-2104.12836-13, arxivID-2212.05561-14}. In this paper, we propose to use contrastive learning to learn a universal user encoder by maximizing the similarity between positive user pairs and minimizing the similarity between negative user pairs. <|section_end|>

