 While Reinforcement Learning (RL) algorithms recently achieved impressive feats across a range of domains  #ref1# #ref2# #ref3#, they remain sample inefficient  #ref4# #ref5# and are therefore of limited use for real-world robotics applications. Intelligent agents during their lifetime discover and reuse skills at multiple levels of behavioural and temporal abstraction to efficiently tackle new situations. For example, in manipulation domains, beneficial abstractions could include low-level  actions, object manipulation, locomotion, navigation, and high-level task plans \cite{arxivID-2012.10147-0}. Hierarchical RL (HRL) and KL-regularized RL (KRL) both provide mechanisms for the discovery and reuse of skills, but they differ in their assumptions and theoretical foundations. HRL assumes a hierarchical structure of the state space, which enables the reuse of skills across multiple levels of abstraction \cite{arxivID-2012.10147-1}. KRL, on the other hand, assumes that the skill distribution is concentrated around a set of expert skills, which enables the reuse of skills across different tasks \cite{arxivID-2110.12997-2}. While both HRL and KRL have been shown to be effective in various domains \cite{arxivID-2006.11704-3, arxivID-2007.15588-4, arxivID-1712.00948-5}, their respective theoretical foundations and assumptions may lead to complementary benefits when combined.

