 Modern generative models like StyleGANv2 can map a random latent code to a photorealistic image, especially in the human face domain. In many application scenarios, to be useful in practice, the process of generation must allow for explicit control of the content. Previous work explored modes of control based on analysis of a pre-trained model and identification of  latent codes that correspond to specific attributes \cite{arxivID-2011.12799-0, arxivID-2004.02546-1, arxivID-2007.06600-2, arxivID-2104.12476-3}. Alternatively, one can directly train a mapping function from a text prompt to a latent code, without requiring access to the pre-trained generator \cite{arxivID-2102.01645-4, arxivID-2304.13742-5, arxivID-2210.02347-6, arxivID-2111.13792-7}. In this work, we propose a hybrid approach, where we train a mapping function from a text prompt to a latent code, but we use the pre-trained generator to generate the image. This approach allows us to leverage the rich latent space of the pre-trained generator, while providing explicit control over the content of the generated image through the text prompt.

One of the main challenges in training a mapping function from a text prompt to a latent code is the lack of paired data, i.e., a set of text prompts and corresponding latent codes. Previous work has addressed this challenge by using a pre-trained CLIP model \cite{arxivID-2103.00020-8} to generate pseudo-labels for a set of images, which are then used as training data for the mapping function \cite{arxivID-2102.01645-9, arxivID-2304.13742-10, arxivID-2210.02347-11, arxivID-2111.13792-12}. However, this approach requires a large set of images to generate sufficient pseudo-labels, which can be difficult to obtain for certain domains. In this work, we propose a different approach, where we use the pre-trained CLIP model to generate pseudo-labels for a set of images that are generated by the pre-trained generator itself. This approach allows us to use a much smaller set of images to generate pseudo-labels, which makes it more feasible to use for domains with limited image data.

Another challenge in training a mapping function from a text prompt to a latent code is the lack of diversity in the generated images. Previous work has addressed this challenge by using a conditional variational autoencoder (CVAE) \cite{arxivID-2102.12037-13}, which allows the mapping function to generate multiple images for a given text prompt. However, this approach can be computationally expensive, as it requires training a separate CVAE for each text prompt. In this work, we propose a different approach, where we use a single CVAE that can generate multiple images for a given text prompt. This approach allows us to use a single CVAE for all text prompts, which makes it more efficient than previous work.

