 The proliferation of Internet-of-Things (IoT) applications, intelligent robots, and Industry 4.0 networks is resulting in unprecedented need for massive and spectrally efficient connections. As part of the sixth-generation (6G) evolution roadmap, extremely reliable and low-latency communication (ERLLC), further-enhanced mobile broadband (FeMBB), and ultra-massive machine-type communication (umMTC) have been proposed to enable envisioned heterogeneous 6G applications  #ref1#. For ubiquitous scenarios of umMTC and ERLLC, where traditional grant-based (GB) transmissions are impractical due to the corresponding excessive signaling overhead and  long latency, semi-grant-free (SGF) transmissions have been proposed \cite{arxivID-1801.04953-0, arxivID-2210.08940-1, arxivID-2002.07842-2, arxivID-2302.12681-3}. In SGF transmissions, the base station (BS) allocates dedicated resources to the grant-based user (GBU) for periodic traffic. The GBU and the grant-free users (GFUs) access the BS through the same resource block (RB). The GBU and GFUs are admitted to transmit their data through SGF transmissions based on the BS's decisions. Due to the lack of the RBs' orthogonality, the GBU and GFUs are subject to intra- and inter-RB interference. To manage the interference and enable SGF transmissions, non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) has been proposed \cite{arxivID-1904.07975-4, arxivID-2011.12388-5, arxivID-2009.00105-6}. However, the interference management in SGF transmissions assisted by NOMA is challenging. The challenge is twofold. First, the GBU is not aware of the GFUs' presence and power levels. Second, the BS does not know the GBU's channel state information (CSI) and the GFUs' power levels. As a result, the GBU's transmission power is not adapted to the GFUs' power levels, and the BS is not able to perform successive interference cancellation (SIC) in the right order. Hence, the GBU's and GFUs' outage performance is degraded.

In this paper, we propose a cognitive radio-inspired rate-splitting multiple access (CR-RSMA) scheme to assist SGF transmissions. In the proposed CR-RSMA-assisted SGF (CR-RSMA-SGF) transmissions, the GBU and admitted GFUs access the BS through the same RB. Using the cognitive radio principle, the GBU and admitted GFUs are treated as the primary and secondary users, respectively. The admitted GFUs' rate-splitting technique is applied to manage the intra- and inter-RB interference and realize SGF transmissions. The admitted GFUs' transmit power allocation, target rate allocation, and SIC decoding order at the BS are jointly optimized to attain the maximum achievable rate for the admitted GFUs without deteriorating the GBU's outage performance compared to orthogonal multiple access (OMA). The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a CR-RSMA scheme to assist SGF transmissions. To the best of our knowledge, the proposed CR-RSMA-SGF scheme is the first attempt to employ rate-splitting to manage the intra- and inter-RB interference in SGF transmissions.
\item We optimize the admitted GFUs' transmit power allocation, target rate allocation, and SIC decoding order at the BS to maximize the admitted GFUs' achievable rate without degrading the GBU's outage performance compared to OMA.
\item We derive exact expressions and asymptotic analysis for the admitted GFUs' outage probability to evaluate the system performance achieved by CR-RSMA-SGF transmissions. We verify the superior outage performance and full multiuser diversity gain achieved by CR-RSMA-SGF transmissions through the analytical and simulation results.
\end{itemize}

