In recent years, many numerical methods have been proposed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-1910.05703-2}, a global optimization-based dimer method was proposed to find saddle points by coupling ant colony optimization algorithm with optimization-based shrinking dimer method. In \cite{arxivID-2208.09273-3}, a local surrogate model was constructed to find saddle points by using global structure search methods. In \cite{arxivID-2010.12107-4}, a metaheuristic hybrid cuckoo search was used to construct super-cell random approximate having targeted atomic site and pair probabilities on arbitrary crystal lattices. In \cite{arxivID-2104.09764-5}, a contact map-based crystal structure prediction method was proposed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2301.12477-6}, a graph reinforcement learning approach was developed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2403.03511-7}, a quality-diversity algorithm was applied to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2207.09971-8}, machine learning models were used to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2107.13522-9}, a feedback loop coupling a neural network with importance sampling was used to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2212.10135-10}, a stochastic algorithm was proposed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-1504.06622-11}, a fixed-point homotopy method was used to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2310.04643-12}, a semi-implicit method was used to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2204.00171-13}, the convergence analysis of saddle dynamics was developed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2310.07108-14}, an efficient saddle search method was proposed to find saddle points. In \cite{arxivID-2011.04869-15}, a projection method was proposed to find saddle points.

In this paper, we consider the following constrained optimization problem
\begin{align}
\min_{u \in \mathbb{R}^n} & \mathcal{E}(u),\label{eq:energy}\\
\text{s.t.} & \mathcal{C}(u) = 0,\label{eq:constraint}
\end{align}
