\subsection{Unconscious Bias}
Unconscious bias, also known as implicit bias, refers to the unconscious and often unintentional attitudes or stereotypes that individuals hold about certain groups of people based on their social or cultural background. These biases can influence how people perceive, think about, and behave towards others without their conscious awareness. Unconscious bias can manifest in various forms, such as racial, gender, age, or ethnic bias, and can impact decision-making, interpersonal relationships, and social interactions.

\subsection{Language Models for Bias Detection}
In recent years, there has been growing interest in using language models for bias detection. Several studies have explored the use of language models to detect biases in text, including gender bias \cite{arxivID-1904.03310-0}, racial bias \cite{arxivID-2109.13137-1}, and a specialized AAE toxic language detection model, in our case AAE-BERT, to achieve a better classification performance and reduced biases than using a single model. This paper uses the HxEnsemble framework to evaluate two language models, one that is not racially primed and another that is racially primed. We hypothesize that the racially primed language model will have less biases towards the AAE dialect since it is more sensitive to the AAE dialect. Since the racial priming is a technique used for annotators, we hypothesize that the racially primed language model will have less biases towards the AAE dialect since it is more sensitive to the AAE dialect. \cite{arxivID-2109.13137-2}, and political bias \cite{arxivID-1905.00724-3}. Language models have also been used to detect biases in other domains, such as text classification \cite{arxivID-2210.11762-4}, natural language inference \cite{arxivID-2105.05541-5}, and dialogue systems \cite{arxivID-1910.10486-6}. These studies have shown the potential of language models to detect biases in text, but also highlight the need for careful evaluation and mitigation of these biases to ensure fairness and equity in their use.

\subsection{Transfer Learning}
Transfer learning is a machine learning technique that involves using knowledge from one domain or task to improve performance on another related domain or task. This approach is particularly useful when there is a scarcity of labeled data in the target domain or task. By leveraging knowledge from a related domain or task, transfer learning can help to improve the accuracy and efficiency of machine learning models, particularly in cases where labeled data is expensive or difficult to obtain. Transfer learning has been applied to a wide range of applications, including computer vision, natural language processing, and speech recognition. In this paper, we will explore the use of transfer learning for bias detection, where the goal is to transfer knowledge from a related domain or task to improve the accuracy of bias detection models. <|section_end|>

