 The idea of recovering blurry images into their sharp version has been presented since a decade ago and remains an active research area in computer vision. The spread of pixels usually causes a blurry image owing to the motion effect during capture time. This motion is modeled by a specific point spread  function (PSF) that is associated with the camera optics and camera sensor. As a result, the blurry image can be mathematically represented by the convolution of the sharp image and the PSF. However, the PSF is unknown in real-world scenarios, making the problem of image deblurring ill-posed. The PSF is only known for synthetic data, which is usually simulated by a Gaussian or a uniform distribution. The blurred image can be represented by a combination of the PSF and the sharp image as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{PSF} \otimes \mathbf{S} + \mathbf{N}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{B}$ is the blurred image, $\mathbf{S}$ is the sharp image, $\mathbf{PSF}$ is the point spread function, $\otimes$ is the convolution operator, and $\mathbf{N}$ is the noise component. The goal of image deblurring is to recover the sharp image $\mathbf{S}$ from the blurred image $\mathbf{B}$ and PSF $\mathbf{PSF}$.

In the past decade, many deblurring methods have been proposed to address this problem. Early attempts of image deblurring are based on the optimization-based method \cite{arxivID-1710.00620-0, arxivID-cs/0609165-1, arxivID-1206.3594-2, arxivID-1704.00331-3, arxivID-1503.04776-4, arxivID-1602.01410-5, arxivID-1609.01380-6, arxivID-1311.4029-7}. These methods rely on the prior knowledge of the blur kernel and the sharp image. However, these methods are computationally expensive and sensitive to the blur kernel.

In recent years, deep learning-based methods have achieved significant progress in image deblurring. These methods utilize a convolutional neural network (CNN) to learn the mapping from the blurry image to the sharp image. The CNN-based methods have shown promising results in various deblurring tasks, including deblurring of the entire image, deblurring of the human face, and deblurring of the human body. For example, Nah et al. \cite{arxivID-1612.02177-8} proposed a multi-scale CNN that can restore sharp images in an end-to-end manner. They also presented a new large-scale dataset that provides pairs of realistic blurry images and their corresponding ground truth sharp images. The proposed model trained on this dataset achieved state-of-the-art performance in dynamic scene deblurring.

