 Collision-free trajectory generation is essential for multi-robot systems to perform various missions in a shared environment, such as cooperative inspection and transportation  #ref1#. However, it becomes especially challenging when a large number of agile robots navigate in a crowded space with high speed. The commonly-seen multi-robot trajectory generation (MATG) algorithms can be classified into roughly six categories: potential fields #ref2# #ref3# #ref4# that design virtual driving forces induced by artificial potentials; geometric guidance #ref5# #ref6# such as reciprocal velocity obstacles (RVO)  #ref6# #ref7# that analyze the geometric properties based on the position and velocity of the others; conflicts resolution  #ref8# #ref9# that rely on a central coordinator to resolve the conflicts; sampling-based methods #ref10# that utilize sampling-based algorithms to generate trajectories; optimization-based methods #ref11# that optimize the trajectories in a centralized manner; and learning-based methods #ref12# that train the model to predict the trajectories of the others.

However, most of these methods lack theoretical guarantees on the feasibility of the underlying optimization, which is crucial for the successful execution of the trajectories in the real world. In addition, when applied in a distributed manner without a central coordinator, deadlocks often occur where several robots block each other indefinitely. Whereas heuristic methods such as introducing random perturbations exist, no profound analyses are given to validate these measures.

