Personal fabrication devices have been made increasingly accessible over time. In the 1980s, the first 3D-printer was invented. This technology was initially used by industry, and was not accessible to the general public. In the 1990s, the first personal 3D-printers were developed, but they were still expensive and difficult to use. In the 2000s, the cost of 3D-printers decreased, and they became more accessible to the general public. In the 2010s, the cost of 3D-printers continued to decrease, and they became even more accessible. Today, 3D-printers are widely available, and can be bought for as little as a few hundred dollars. 

This trend of simplification has been driven by two main factors: technological advancements and user expectations. Technological advancements have made it possible to create more complex and accurate artifacts with less effort. User expectations have also played a role, as users have become accustomed to being able to create anything they want, whenever they want. 

\subsection{In-Situ Fabrication}
\label{sec:in_situ}

The trend of simplifying the fabrication process has led to a new trend of in-situ fabrication. In-situ fabrication refers to the process of fabricating an object in the same location where it will be used. This trend has been amplified by emerging paradigms of mobile, wearable, and body-integrated fabrication.

