 Recommender systems (RS) aim to estimate user preferences on items that users have not yet seen. Progress in deep learning (DL) has spawned a wide range of novel and complex neural recommendation models. Many improvements have been achieved in prior literature, however, much of them performs evaluation  on small-scale datasets, which may not be able to fully evaluate the models in large-scale real-world scenarios. To the best of our knowledge, most of the existing benchmark datasets for RS either are created at a small scale or involve very limited forms of user feedback. For example, the popular MovieLens dataset \cite{arxivID-1404.1653-0}, which is widely used in the literature of collaborative filtering (CF) \cite{arxivID-2012.03433-1}, has only 10K users and 100K items. Other datasets, such as the Amazon dataset \cite{arxivID-1506.08839-2}, the Yelp dataset \cite{arxivID-1712.04350-3}, and the LastFM dataset \cite{arxivID-1307.7142-4}, also only contain very limited forms of user feedback. The lack of large-scale and diverse datasets may not be able to fully evaluate the practical values of RS models in real-world scenarios.

