In the literature, video summarization has been studied from two different perspectives, namely, \textit{clips-based} and \textit{frames-based} \cite{arxivID-2210.11707-1}. The former aims to generate a summary by selecting a subset of shots/clips from the original video. The latter is to generate a summary by selecting a subset of frames from the original video. In this paper, we focus on the latter.

The existing methods for frames-based video summarization can be roughly divided into two categories: \textit{supervised} and \textit{unsupervised} \cite{arxivID-2101.06072-2}. The supervised methods usually need to collect a large number of human annotations for a training dataset, and then train a supervised model to predict the importance score for each frame in a video. The unsupervised methods, on the other hand, do not require human annotations, and can be trained in an end-to-end manner. In general, the supervised methods usually achieve better performance than the unsupervised ones, because the supervised methods can learn from human annotations and thus generate more accurate importance scores. However, it is extremely expensive and difficult to collect human annotations for large-scale video datasets. As a result, the unsupervised methods have attracted increasing attention recently.

In this paper, we propose a convolutional attentive adversarial network (CAAN) for video summarization. The key idea is to build a deep summarizer in an unsupervised way. Upon the generative adversarial network \cite{arxivID-1406.2661-3}, our overall framework consists of a generator and a discriminator. The former predicts importance scores for all frames of a video while the latter tries to distinguish the score-weighted frame features from original frame features. Specifically, the generator employs a fully convolutional sequence network to extract global representation of a video, and an attention-based network to output normalized importance scores. To learn the parameters, our objective function is composed of three loss functions, which can guide the frame-level importance score prediction collaboratively. To validate this proposed method, we have conducted extensive experiments on two public benchmarks SumMe \cite{arxivID-1712.06899-4} and TVSum \cite{arxivID-1809.08854-5}. The results show the superiority of our proposed method against other state-of-the-art unsupervised approaches. Our method even outperforms some published supervised approaches. An example of video summarization is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:example}.

