\subsection{Supervised Methods}
The supervised methods for video summarization can be roughly divided into two categories: \textit{feature-based} and \textit{model-based} \cite{arxivID-2101.06072-6}. The feature-based methods first extract some hand-crafted features from each frame of a video, and then train a supervised model to predict the importance score for each frame based on these features. The model-based methods usually build a supervised model directly on raw video frames, and then use this model to predict the importance score for each frame. The main advantage of the supervised methods is that they can learn from human annotations and thus generate more accurate importance scores. However, it is extremely expensive and difficult to collect human annotations for large-scale video datasets. As a result, the supervised methods have not been widely used in practice.

