However, conventional analog computing is mainly in a mixed-signal manner. In the mixed-signal computing paradigm, analog computing is coupled with digital control circuits. To realize the full potential of analog computing, it is critical to reduce the overhead of digital control circuits. As a result, a number of analog computing architectures have been proposed, such as in-memory computing \cite{arxivID-2302.06417-4} and digital-to-analog converters (DACs)-free computing \cite{arxivID-2008.00572-5}. However, the analog-to-digital (A/D) conversion, which is the key component of digital control circuits, still limits the overall systems energy efficiency. To address this issue, we propose to devise an efficient analog activation unit with magnetic tunnel junction (MTJ)-based analog content-addressable memory (MACAM) \cite{arxivID-1907.08177-6}, simultaneously realizing nonlinear activation and A/D conversion in a fused fashion. Compared to conventional activation implementations, our proposed activation unit can achieve a significant reduction of A/D conversion energy cost. To compensate for the nascent and therefore currently limited representation capability of MACAM, we propose to mix our analog activation unit with digital activation dataflow. A fully differential framework, SuperMixer, is developed to search for an optimized activation workload assignment, adaptive to various activation energy constraints. The effectiveness of our proposed methods is evaluated on a silicon photonic accelerator \cite{arxivID-2102.06960-7}. Compared to standard activation implementation, our mixed activation system with the searched assignment can achieve competitive accuracy with $40\%$ energy saving on A/D conversion and activation. <|section_end|>

 Changes in the world are often dominated by the dynamics of a relatively small number of latent variables. Identifying these variables is useful for making predictions. For example, in video prediction, the task is to predict  the next frame of a video. Videos often have a small number of dominant features (e.g. moving cars, people, etc.). In this paper, we describe a model that predicts these features and uses them to select the next frame of a video. This prediction is done without training a generative model of the videos.

In general, the prediction of future images can be divided into two steps: (1) finding a latent representation of the current image and (2) predicting the next latent state and decoding it into an image. We propose a model that uses a latent representation to predict the next frame without training a generative model. Our model is a combination of a latent variable model and a classifier. The latent variable model learns the distribution of the latent variables, and the classifier predicts the next latent variable. The latent variable model is trained using contrastive predictive coding (CPC) \cite{arxivID-1807.03748-0}, a method for learning representations from data by predicting the future in latent space. The classifier is trained using supervised learning, with labels obtained by applying a deterministic function to the latent variables. We show that this model can be used to solve the following tasks:
\begin{itemize}
\item Stochastic video prediction: the task is to predict the next frame of a video with stochastic dynamics.
\item Anomaly detection: the task is to detect anomalies in a video.
\item Sequence completion: the task is to complete a sequence of images with a single image that is consistent with the sequence.
\end{itemize}

We also propose a new method for training latent variable models, called Markov Contrastive Predictive Coding (M-CPC). The main difference between M-CPC and CPC is that M-CPC uses a Markov model to predict the future in latent space, while CPC uses a fully-connected model. We show that M-CPC is more efficient than CPC for predicting the next frame of a video, and that it can be used to solve the sequence completion task.

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a new model for predicting future images that uses a latent variable model and a classifier. The model is trained using contrastive predictive coding and supervised learning.
\item We propose a new method for training latent variable models, called Markov Contrastive Predictive Coding (M-CPC). We show that M-CPC is more efficient than CPC for predicting the next frame of a video.
\item We demonstrate the usefulness of our model by solving the following tasks: stochastic video prediction, anomaly detection, and sequence completion.
\end{itemize}

In Section 2, we review related work. In Section 3, we describe our model and training method. In Section 4, we present experimental results. In Section 5, we discuss the limitations of our model and future work. In Section 6, we conclude.
Related Work
\subsection{Contrastive Predictive Coding}

Contrastive Predictive Coding (CPC) \cite{arxivID-1807.03748-1} is a method for learning representations from data by predicting the future in latent space. CPC is a special case of predictive coding \cite{arxivID-1412.7955-2}, a theory of the brain that posits that the brain learns by predicting the future. CPC is related to other methods for learning representations from data, such as autoencoders \cite{arxivID-2003.05991-3} and generative adversarial networks \cite{arxivID-1406.2661-4}. However, CPC is different from these methods in that it uses a contrastive loss, which is a type of loss function that is used to train models by comparing the output of the model to a set of target values. The contrastive loss is a special case of the cross-entropy loss, which is a type of loss function that is used to train models by comparing the output of the model to a set of target values. The contrastive loss is a special case of the cross-entropy loss, which is a type of loss function that is used to train models by comparing the output of the model to a set of target values.

\subsection{Video Prediction}

Video prediction is the task of predicting the future frames of a video. This task is important for many applications, such as autonomous driving, robotics, and video compression. There are two main approaches to video prediction: generative models and discriminative models. Generative models learn a probability distribution over the future frames of a video, while discriminative models learn a function that maps the current frame of a video to the future frames. Generative models are more difficult to train than discriminative models, but they can generate more diverse and realistic future frames. Discriminative models are easier to train than generative models, but they can only predict future frames that are similar to the training data.

\subsection{Anomaly Detection}

Anomaly detection is the task of identifying patterns in data that do not conform to expected behavior. This task is important for many applications, such as fraud detection, network security, and medical diagnosis. There are two main approaches to anomaly detection: supervised learning and unsupervised learning. Supervised learning requires labeled data, while unsupervised learning does not. Supervised learning is more accurate than unsupervised learning, but it requires more data. Unsupervised learning is more efficient than supervised learning, but it can only detect anomalies that are similar to the training data.

\subsection{Sequence Completion}

Sequence completion is the task of predicting the next item in a sequence. This task is important for many applications, such as natural language processing, speech recognition, and image classification. There are two main approaches to sequence completion: generative models and discriminative models. Generative models learn a probability distribution over the next item in a sequence, while discriminative models learn a function that maps a sequence to the next item. Generative models are more difficult to train than discriminative models, but they can generate more diverse and realistic predictions. Discriminative models are easier to train than generative models, but they can only predict items that are similar to the training data. <|section_end|>

